\cf2 \cb5 That's meaningful evidence of a growing audience interested in some of the formats you intend to produce.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But I wouldn't interpret the numbers as proof that an unknown, quiet, intellectually unconventional streamer will grow faster there.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Large creators account for a substantial share of livestream attention. Kick's category-based discovery may offer opportunities, but it can also bury a new broadcaster under established personalities.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Meanwhile, YouTube offers something fundamentally different: the possibility of a livestream, its archived recording, a later clip, or a short explanatory video bringing in viewers long after the original session has ended.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 I view Kick as an additional live community and monetization experiment; YouTube as the primary discovery, archival, and long-form publication system.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 There's little reason to force yourself to choose between those functions if distributing the same signal to both is sufficiently inexpensive.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 3. Three important catches in the fine print\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 These are the things I'd want to understand before building the production around Kick.\cf0 \cb1 \

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 Kick's high-paying Partner Program reduces its stream-based payout by 50% during simultaneous broadcasts to other long-form platforms.
\f0\b0  Partners must enable the multistream setting when broadcasting to YouTube or Twitch. This is a constraint on the higher Partner earning arrangement, not a stated reduction of the ordinary 95/5 subscription split. \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

