\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 \strokec2 One hundred and six paying Kick subscribers is not a trivial target for an unknown broadcaster. It is, however, a comprehensible economic milestone.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And there is no reason those 106 supporters would all have to come from Kick.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You could assemble the monthly support through some combination of Patreon memberships, Kick subscriptions, one-time contributions, and eventual YouTube native monetization.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 This makes a multisurface approach more attractive than expecting one platform's algorithm to deliver everything.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I'd preserve your existing Patreon philosophy\'97
\f1\i Same adventure. Different bill
\f0\i0 \'97across the platforms. Let viewers support through the mechanism most comfortable to them. A Kick subscription may automatically include a badge or emotes, but it doesn't need to buy special personal access, control over research priorities, or exclusive ownership of the work.\cf0 \cb1 \
