\f4\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 7. The economic model I'd favor\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 This is where I think your aborted thought about dual monetization was heading in a useful direction.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 You don't need to choose between Patreon and platform-native support.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I would distinguish between the 
\f3\b participation relationship
\f0\b0  and the 
\f3\b payment rails
\f0\b0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Patreon can remain your enduring membership and project-support venue.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Kick subscriptions and YouTube memberships can serve people who prefer supporting creators inside the platform where they're watching.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 One-time contributions can accommodate people who don't want recurring payments.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And all of those can fund the same public work without granting fundamentally different rights or levels of participation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The economic benefit isn't merely that one system charges lower fees than another.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 It's that you're removing avoidable friction for people who already want to help.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 For example, somebody watching on Kick might subscribe with two clicks rather than navigate to Patreon, create or authenticate an account, locate your tier, and complete a separate payment.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That convenience can matter more than a modest difference in platform fees.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 At the same time, I would avoid asking everyone to subscribe on multiple platforms. Give people convenient choices, not overlapping obligations.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
