\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 \strokec2 I would still favor your YouTube + Kick simulcast experiment.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But I would adjust the specific hypothesis:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 Kick isn't necessarily where you'll find a large preexisting audience of people who enjoy watching programmers program. It may be where you discover people who enjoy spending time with an unusual personality who happens to be programming, inventing, and exploring things.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 That's a more promising interpretation of the audience opportunity.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And I'd make one additional experiment part of the launch: spend a few sessions browsing the Software Development category yourself, at different times of day, and occasionally interact as a genuine viewer.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Watch how other developers talk to chat, how much actual programming they do, whether people return, and whether the broadcaster's personality or technical subject seems to be the main attraction.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That would give you a much better sense of Kick's small technical culture than watching its biggest celebrities.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 My central conclusion is that 
\f3\b Kick's coding scene is real, but presently small enough that you shouldn't expect it to supply your breakout audience by itself.
\f0\b0  Its broader personality-streaming audience is the more interesting growth opportunity, while YouTube remains your stronger platform for making technical work independently discoverable.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And you don't have to become N3on to participate in it. 
\f2 \uc0\u55357 \u56834 \u9774 \u65039 
\f0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

