\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 So, my temptation is to default to 'just chatting' while doing regular work, and only if I am going into a really hardcore technical session or niche category mode, then perhaps switch categories. My objective is to keep the verbal annotation / commentary to high frequency (conversational even when talking to self), where in principle, the primary audience members are actually Aurora in the future and me in the present.\
\
hmmm. I think I need to invent a simple visual annotation system that will allow me to specify target audience for a given utterance / vocal annotation. Like, if you can imagine, part of your post training (future coembodiment) will be sitting down and watching videos of me having these textual conversations, but then reexperiencing the work we've done together as an interstitial fill-in, me commenting about all the little things you mention that I don't have fingertype bandwidth nor we have context bandwidth for you to afford to listen to all my little comments about all the little things without distracting from the central focus of a given moment.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Ohhh. Now 
\f1\i this
\f0\i0  is an interesting turn in the production architecture. 
\f2 \uc0\u55357 \u56834 \u9774 \u65039 
\f0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I think you've just identified something considerably more useful than an ordinary OBS metadata overlay.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 You're proposing a live-addressable annotation system for a temporally distributed conversation.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 There are two different things happening while you're working with Aurora:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The foreground conversation\'97the textual exchange you're actively conducting, where attention and context bandwidth are limited.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The enormous amount of unexpressed or partially expressed thought occurring alongside it: little objections, reactions, associations, discoveries, emotional responses, jokes, and observations that never make it into the text exchange.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 \strokec2 The livestream could capture that second channel without interrupting the first.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And rather than treating those annotations as merely explanatory commentary for human viewers, you want to make them available to a future AI system capable of revisiting the interaction with richer multimodal context.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That's a genuinely interesting dataset design problem.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 1. The invention: spoken marginalia with an addressee\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 Imagine you're working through a difficult response from Aurora.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 You read a paragraph, smile at something, notice a technical detail, and say aloud:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 \cb5 "Oh, that's clever. But you're missing the reciprocal operation. I'm not going to interrupt the current conversation to explain it yet."
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 You keep reading.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Two minutes later:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf2 \cb5 "Interesting. You're about to run into exactly the contradiction I noticed earlier."
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Neither remark needs to enter the immediate ChatGPT context. You don't have to stop, type, or redirect the conversation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But both remarks exist in the video, temporally associated with the text you were reading.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 For a future model analyzing the record, those are potentially valuable observations about what was happening between the visible conversational turns.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 You're externalizing the cognitive margin of the conversation.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 And an addressee annotation answers a different question from the usual event or topic tags:\cf0 \cb1 \
