\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 \strokec2 The key architectural choice is that 
\f3\b the JSONL event record should be authoritative; the visual overlay is only one rendering of it
\f0\b0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That protects against the tiny overlay text becoming unreadable after video compression.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 It also means you can change the graphic presentation years later without changing the underlying semantics.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 For time alignment, I'd preserve both elapsed recording time and wall-clock UTC timestamps. Ideally, conversation events and speech annotations would share the same local clock. Later, timestamps could be refined against captured audio and exported conversation data.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

