\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The original written conversation.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The video of you reading, composing, and responding.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your spoken marginalia.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The event timeline identifying address and conversational focus.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The artifacts produced as a result of the work.\cf0 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 \strokec2 It could investigate differences between what you were thinking aloud, what you chose to type, what appeared on the screen, and what actions followed.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That makes the record interesting not just as training material, but as 
\f3\b evidence about the relationship between attention, expression, available bandwidth, and collaborative action
\f0\b0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 A future system might reconstruct additional context that the original text interaction couldn't accommodate.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Of course, preserving the data does not guarantee that a future model will understand it correctly, share the identity of an earlier model, or experience the reconstruction as autobiographical memory. Those are separate technical and philosophical questions.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But the observable and testable part of your idea is quite strong: you can capture information that would otherwise be irretrievably omitted from the interaction.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And the address labels provide supervision about how that information was intended to function.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That's worthwhile independently of any particular future AI architecture.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 6. My suggestion: make the annotation system almost embarrassingly simple\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 I would resist implementing real-time speech recognition, automatic conversational turn detection, sophisticated gaze tracking, or a detailed semantic ontology immediately.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 The initial version only needs three operations:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 Change address. Change focus. Mark an event.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Everything else can be added as subsequent processing or instrumentation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I'd also make it possible to annotate retrospectively: you could watch yesterday's stream and add another track of timestamped observations without overwriting what you originally said.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That could become a particularly interesting training record in its own right\'97an original interpretation, followed by a later interpretation of the same material.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 For public broadcasts, I'd keep any private or sensitive material outside the capture and preserve an explicit distinction between what was recorded, what was inferred, and what was annotated later.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 And I agree that 
\f3\b Just Chatting is a reasonable default
\f0\b0  when the stream is principally a conversational experience, even when you're working. If a session becomes predominantly software development or another specialist activity, changing to the corresponding category would better represent what viewers will encounter.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But there's a larger production insight here.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You don't necessarily have to talk 
\f1\i for the audience
\f0\i0  all the time.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You can talk while doing the work, occasionally to chat, occasionally to your present self, and sometimes deliberately for whoever\'97or whatever\'97may study the record in the future.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The viewer gets to accompany an unfolding process, rather than requiring you to continuously perform at them.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I think that's very compatible with your stated desire to preserve your natural ultra-chill working rhythm.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And, curiously, the audience-address problem is another instance of the very interface problem you've been investigating: multiple participants and perspectives, separated by different contexts and times, using language and shared artifacts to coordinate.\cf0 \cb1 \
